{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"scenario_analysis (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "scenarios": [
  {
   "case": "Bull",
   "probability": "25%",
   "driver": "The AI upgrade cycle and the foldable Duo extend the iPhone cycle into FY2027-28; services growth stays above 15%; gross margin holds 48% through the memory cycle; the search payment survives at a lower rate.",
   "evidence": "iPhone revenue rose 22.4% in nine months; iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max list at $1,199 and $1,299; the foldable Duo launched 2026-09-09; IDC expects more than 17M foldable iPhones by 2027 at a $2,550+ ASP [S6, S113, S84].",
   "outcome": "FY2027 EPS of $10.20 at 35x gives $357, +4% price and about +4.3% total return over twelve months; the DCF at an 8.5% cost of equity on a 35% margin and 14% initial growth is $277; a three-year IRR of up to +9% needs a 34x exit.",
   "implication": "Own it, but the upside comes from a multiple that stays elevated, not from a margin of safety at entry."
  },
  {
   "case": "Base",
   "probability": "50%",
   "driver": "Growth fades from 9% to 5%, gross margin normalises to 47-48% as memory costs reset, services grows 12-14%, and the search payment is renegotiated lower.",
   "evidence": "The DCF is $212 at a 9.0% cost of equity on a 33% margin and 9% initial growth; the 31.0x four-year mean P/E implies $303 on FY2027 EPS of $9.79; revenue compounded 3.3% a year FY2021-25 and 5.0% to the last twelve months [S6, S10, S7, S97].",
   "outcome": "$284 at 29x FY2027 EPS of $9.79, -18% price and -17.7% total return; the DCF is $212 at 9.0% (33% margin) and $277 at 8.5% (35%); the base-case rate grid gives $229 at 8.5%/4.0%; the three-year IRR is 1.3% at a 31x exit.",
   "implication": "Do not own at $344.63: the compounding is real but already paid for, and the three-year return is roughly the shareholder yield."
  },
  {
   "case": "Bear",
   "probability": "25%",
   "driver": "Memory costs break the hardware margin below 46%, services growth falls under 8% as the DMA and link-out remedies bite, and the search payment is cut or banned on appeal.",
   "evidence": "The iPhone 18 Pro bill of materials is up 38% year on year with memory 34% of it; the Ninth Circuit affirmed civil contempt on 2025-12-11; the DOJ cross-appeal seeks a payment ban; losing $20B is 12.9% of EPS [S99, S79, S76, S6].",
   "outcome": "$241 at 24.6x FY2027 EPS of $9.79, a 30% drawdown; the $133 DCF at a 9.5% cost of equity on 30% margins and 4% growth is the permanent-loss tail; three-year IRR -12% at a 27x exit.",
   "implication": "The entry price has to price this: even from $270 the three-year return is only 5.0-13.3% across 27-34x exits, so size, not conviction, carries the risk."
  }
 ],
 "assumptions": [
  {
   "assumption": "Gross margin normalises to 47-48%, not the 50.1% June-quarter print, as tariff refunds lapse and memory costs reset.",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "A December 2026 quarter guided at or above 49% gross margin with FY2027 memory contracted at fixed prices.",
   "impact": "-6.0% EPS per 200 basis points of gross margin, and multiple compression on a peak-earnings read [S6, S17].",
   "early_warning": "The 2026-11-02 guidance and TrendForce bill-of-materials data, where the iPhone 18 Pro BOM is up 38% with memory at 34% of it [S99, S72]."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The Google default-search payment survives the D.C. Circuit appeal, renegotiated lower rather than banned.",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "An appellate ruling banning payments or capping them below the current economics.",
   "impact": "A ban is -12.9% of EPS and about 15% of FY2025 operating income; a materially lower rate resets the services multiple [S6, S80].",
   "early_warning": "D.C. Circuit briefing and decision expected in 2027; DOJ and state cross-appeal filed February 2026 [S76, S80]."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Services grows 12-14% without relying on hardware mix, and the EU take-rate cut does not accelerate.",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "Two consecutive quarters of services growth below 10%, or faster migration to external distribution in the EU after 2026-10-01.",
   "impact": "A 3-point cut in services growth removes roughly a tenth of the sum-of-the-parts value; each $1B of EU commission lost is $0.75B of gross profit [S78].",
   "early_warning": "The quarterly services line and developer take-up of the 5% Core Technology Commission [S77, S6]."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The buyback continues at $80B a year or more with net cash positive, so EPS growth keeps its 2-3 point financial tailwind.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "disproof": "A cut to the authorisation, or repurchases funded by new debt.",
   "impact": "Buybacks added 2.4 points of the 22.4% nine-month EPS growth; losing that cuts EPS growth to mid-single digits [S6, S17].",
   "early_warning": "The May 2027 authorisation and the FY2026 10-K cash-flow statement [S5, S6]."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The Gemini licence stays an R&D expense and is not repriced per device or per query into services margin.",
   "confidence": "low",
   "disproof": "Renewal terms disclosed that price the model per device or per query.",
   "impact": "A $1B annual licence is 0.65% of TTM operating income and $0.06 of EPS; per-device pricing at $0.50/$1/$2 across 2.5B devices is $1.25B/$2.5B/$5.0B a year, $0.07/$0.14/$0.28 of EPS [S111, S73].",
   "early_warning": "Renewal commentary from Apple or Google, and any services margin step-down unexplained by mix [S111]."
  }
 ],
 "consensus": {
  "consensus": "The market pays 39.5x trailing and 35.2x forward earnings. Our reading is that the price capitalises the 2026 margin as a base; that is an assumption about what the price discounts, not a sourced consensus estimate, because no dated consensus gross-margin or group-revenue figure is in the register. The only consensus in the register is iPhone revenue: $252.4B for FY2026 (+20.4%) and $277.4B for FY2027 (+9.9%).",
  "our_view": "Non-consensus on price, not on franchise: the business is a genuine compounder, but the 2026 margin is a peak and no point of the DCF grid reaches $344.63. The evidence supports a watchlist place and an entry near $270, not ownership at the current multiple.",
  "evidence": "The DCF is $277 at 8.5% on a 35% margin, $212 at 9.0% on 33% and $133 at 9.5% on 30%; the base-case rate grid gives $229 at 8.5%/4.0% and $193 at 9.5%/4.0%; the sum of the parts is $177-238; and the June-quarter margin carried 2 points of tariff refunds [S6, S10, S71, S72, S17].",
  "proof_event": "The December-quarter gross-margin guide on 2026-11-02, and the D.C. Circuit decision on the Google payments expected in 2027."
 },
 "one_question": {
  "question": "Will the Google default-search payment survive the D.C. Circuit appeal, and at what annual rate?",
  "why": "The payment, put at $20B in 2022 by the trial record, is 18.3% of FY2025 services revenue and about 15% of FY2025 operating income at near-pure margin. A ban or a materially lower rate resets the services multiple that carries most of the valuation, and it moves fair value from the base case toward the bear case regardless of how well the hardware cycle trades."
 },
 "modules": {
  "analogs": "",
  "capital_efficiency": "",
  "position_sizing": "Watchlist, zero position today. First tranche 0.75-1.0% of the fund at $270 or below, where a 9% three-year return becomes reachable across 27-34x exits; second tranche to 2.5-3.0% at $245; cap the position at 5% below $225. Maximum loss at the cap is 1.5% of the fund on the 30% drawdown; the full Kelly at $270 on the stated inputs is 337% of the fund, so the 5% cap is 1.5% of full Kelly and a risk-budget decision. Sell review above $380 (38.8x the FY2027 build); sell on valuation alone above 45x forward, about $440; sell outright on two quarters of sub-10% services growth, gross margin below 47%, or a structure that removes the search payment."
 },
 "author": "Lead Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-31T06:01:13+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

